If Stormbringer and the MKoT are the only two (non
invocation) artifacts in the game you have a 2/3 chance of
getting another artifact (provided the blessed magic lamp
grants a wish), less if there are more artifacts. If those
odds don't daunt you then I'd go for Grayswindir. Frost
Brand loses a lot of it's punch on Astral where many
monsters are cold resistant.

> So far, Iros is skilled in twoweapon, dagger, short sword
> (wasted, that), broad sword (pretty much wasted, too) and long
> sword.  Saber is unskilled.  Twoweapon, dagger and saber can all
> be raised if I level up.

Looking at your skill situation, I might not go for
Grayswindir after all.

So you've spent 3, 2, 2, 3, 3 and earned 13. If you raise
two-weapon and dagger to expert and saber to skilled you
need 9 more levels and you haven't saved anything for
spellcasting. 

By the time you get to the end game your XL is high enough
to overcome all the "to hit" adjustments such that you never
miss. The damage advantage of expert two weapon is 1 point
per hit. The advantage of basic saber is that it avoids a 2
point penalty and at skilled, gains a 1 point bonus. ISTR
that expert at dagger not only pushes the bonus to +2 but
allows the possibility of throwing another dagger.

I would spend 3 slots on expert dagger and enchant and bless
a stack of a dozen or more for throwing. I would use an
artifact long sword as my primary weapon if I could get one,
two weaponed with another long sword or katana. The silver
would come out for a concentration of silver haters, like
Ghost Town. I would spend 3 skill slots to get skilled in
detection spells and use magic mapping, detect treasure and
identify extensively. Light, a matter spell, and the escape
spells jump and speed can be very handy but can be used
unskilled.

If I got Grayswindir I would advance saber to basic and make
it my primary weapon. So 3 for dagger, 3 for detect treasure
and maybe one for saber means you need XL 20 or 21, workable
levels. Conventional wisdom seems to be that advancing most
characters past that brings in too many high level monsters
to make it worthwhile.
